1
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:14,180
Welcome. My name is Samuel Smith. I'm the
creator of the KERI suite of protocols.

2
00:00:15,980 --> 00:00:22,800
And this video is a tutorial talking about
KERI from a high level. KERI stands for

3
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:27,920
Key Event Receipt Infrastructure. It's
been in development for several years now.

4
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:34,040
And you can think of it and as a secure
identifier overlay as a trust spanning

5
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:40,360
layer for the internet. So, we're going to
talk about what that means today. For

6
00:00:40,360 --> 00:00:47,420
resources, you can go to the website KERI
.one. Should be easy to remember. Look at

7
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:54,440
the resources page. There is an active
KERI community with various standards and

8
00:00:54,440 --> 00:01:03,200
specifications. You can find the code,
which is is Apache 2 licensed at the Web

9
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:09,560
of Trust GitHub repo. The Trust Over IP
Foundation, which is a member of the Linux

10
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:16,020
Foundation's decentralized trust
organization, governs the specifications

11
00:01:16,020 --> 00:01:22,400
for the relevant protocols. These are
licensed using the Open Web Foundation

12
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:29,840
license, which is a freely licensed open
specification license. And you can find

13
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:35,320
meetings there and there are a couple of
links for that. The motivation for KERI

14
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:42,680
came from years of working with protocols.
I started life as an academic, did a lot

15
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:52,380
of work with AI and autonomous vehicle
systems back in the 90s. Started working

16
00:01:52,380 --> 00:01:59,760
with protocols in the Internet of Things
also back in the 90s. And later in my

17
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:05,340
career, started working on cloud-based
automation systems and was confronted with

18
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:15,060
the problem that the internet did not have
a really good security system. And if you

19
00:02:15,060 --> 00:02:21,160
think about it, the internet has lots of
problems today and they're getting worse.

20
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:27,580
And so this idea that the internet was
broken in can be traced back to the fact

21
00:02:27,580 --> 00:02:32,620
that the internet never had a security
layer. If you look at the comparison of

22
00:02:32,620 --> 00:02:39,720
the OSI model and the IP model, the layers
where you do authentication are in the

23
00:02:39,720 --> 00:02:46,260
presentation and session layers and the IP
protocol doesn't have those layers. The

24
00:02:46,260 --> 00:02:53,500
result is that instead we use bolt-on
security systems or sometimes called

25
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:59,980
security overlays These are commonly known
as domain name systems, certificate

26
00:02:59,980 --> 00:03:06,080
authorities, openID connect, OAuth,
various types of systems that provide

27
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:15,460
security. And that security is based on
what was a good idea 20 or 30 years ago

28
00:03:15,460 --> 00:03:21,600
when it was created, but now is no longer
such a good idea. And so KERI is about

29
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:27,220
reimagining what the internet would look
like if we had a security layer that

30
00:03:27,220 --> 00:03:29,160
actually worked.

31
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:38,420
The idea can be traced back to something
called a spanning layer. So, the internet

32
00:03:38,420 --> 00:03:45,120
in its early days wasn't viewed by
everyone as something that was going to be

33
00:03:45,120 --> 00:03:51,400
universal. It was an experimental
protocol. But very quickly, relatively

34
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:57,720
speaking, it dominated all of the other
communication protocols and became the

35
00:03:57,720 --> 00:04:07,500
dominant protocol for the whole world. And
one of the analyses as to why that

36
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:13,100
happened is that the internet had a
spanning layer and that spanning layer

37
00:04:13,100 --> 00:04:22,160
made it possible for the internet protocol
to grow in a way that allowed it to

38
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:25,520
subsume all the other protocols and
provide all the services. And that what

39
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:31,820
that spanning layer in the internet is the
IPv4 for, you know, IP version 4 and now

40
00:04:31,820 --> 00:04:38,320
we have IPv6. But originally this is the
diagram that was used to demonstrate that

41
00:04:38,320 --> 00:04:42,280
there was something called a spanning
layer. And if you think about it, it looks

42
00:04:42,280 --> 00:04:46,740
like an hourglass and the waist of the
hourglass is the IP layer. This is the

43
00:04:46,740 --> 00:04:52,720
addressing layer. Everything above that
layer uses the same address and everything

44
00:04:52,720 --> 00:04:57,820
below that provides support for that
addressing layer and that feature of the

45
00:04:57,820 --> 00:05:01,920
spanning layer. And you can look at some
of the papers that have been written about

46
00:05:01,920 --> 00:05:08,020
that is reputed to be the reason one of
the reasons the internet became so

47
00:05:08,020 --> 00:05:15,300
successful as a protocol. The problem that
we've had is that because the spanning

48
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:19,820
layer of the internet did not have any
security built into it, it didn't have a

49
00:05:19,820 --> 00:05:25,720
trust basis. What has happened is that we
now have what I call platform lock trust.

50
00:05:25,960 --> 00:05:30,680
In order to have trust, you have this IP
spanning layer and then you have supports

51
00:05:30,680 --> 00:05:34,520
for it. And then you have applications
built on top of the spanning layer, but

52
00:05:34,520 --> 00:05:40,200
they don't have trust in them. And then
you bolt on trust above those. And so what

53
00:05:40,200 --> 00:05:49,260
you get is trust domain based segmentation
of the upper layers. And so you don't have

54
00:05:49,260 --> 00:06:00,380
trust across those those walled gardens or
columns above that. And so you don't have

55
00:06:00,380 --> 00:06:06,540
trust that spans the internet. It just
spans a given platform. So you get a

56
00:06:06,540 --> 00:06:11,760
bifurcated internet trust map. There's no
trust spanning layer in the current

57
00:06:11,760 --> 00:06:17,020
internet. And you might think of this as
like, you know, different platforms that

58
00:06:17,020 --> 00:06:22,880
have different trust bases or, you know,
and often trust is the identity system,

59
00:06:23,020 --> 00:06:28,100
right? So you have a Facebook identity
system and within Facebook you have trust

60
00:06:28,100 --> 00:06:32,860
and on Google you have its identity
systems and within Google you have trust

61
00:06:32,860 --> 00:06:38,360
and you might have other platforms like
blockchains that have trust within their

62
00:06:38,360 --> 00:06:43,540
trust domain but they're not
interoperable. You can't move trust

63
00:06:43,540 --> 00:06:51,020
between those trust domains. So the basis
of the spanning layer, there's a really

64
00:06:51,020 --> 00:06:58,320
well-written paper that talks about the
hourglass approach to building protocols

65
00:06:58,320 --> 00:07:03,620
where you have a spanning layer. And so we
feel that that's the right approach, but

66
00:07:03,620 --> 00:07:08,040
we just need to have a trust spanning
layer. And we don't want to reinvent a new

67
00:07:08,040 --> 00:07:16,080
internet that would be ludicrous, but we
want to allow the internet to remain the

68
00:07:16,080 --> 00:07:21,620
internet, but add a spanning layer that
allows trust. So there's not locked to

69
00:07:21,620 --> 00:07:27,440
different platforms. And so our solution
we call a waste and a neck is we have the

70
00:07:27,440 --> 00:07:31,980
IP spanning layer, which is the waste, and
then we add a trust spanning layer that is

71
00:07:31,980 --> 00:07:37,500
the neck. And if all trust spanned
applications use the same trust spanning

72
00:07:37,500 --> 00:07:41,900
layer, then they'll have interoperability
in terms of their trust. And so you can

73
00:07:41,900 --> 00:07:46,940
still have platforms, you can still have
all the protocols down below, but if you

74
00:07:46,940 --> 00:07:52,460
want to have applications that span trust,
where trust spans across the applications,

75
00:07:52,980 --> 00:07:56,260
then we want to have a trust spanning
layer. So that's the design goal of KERI.

76
00:07:56,780 --> 00:08:03,200
So I've used the term trust basis or trust
domain a lot. And so I just want to define

77
00:08:03,200 --> 00:08:07,660
it so that you understand what I'm talking
about. A trust basis is the set of trust

78
00:08:07,660 --> 00:08:11,680
roots. And when we talk about systems
design, we talk about things like roots of

79
00:08:11,680 --> 00:08:18,980
trust, things that you can rely on that
have certain well-defined properties. And

80
00:08:18,980 --> 00:08:23,300
then based on those things, you can build
other services and functions on top of

81
00:08:23,300 --> 00:08:30,520
that. So we talk about a trust basis and
then a trust domain is the ecosystems of

82
00:08:30,520 --> 00:08:36,720
interactions that rely on a given trust
basis. So for a trust spanning layer, we

83
00:08:36,720 --> 00:08:43,220
would need to have everything above that
rely on the trust basis that is the

84
00:08:43,220 --> 00:08:49,720
spanning layer. And so KERI is about
defining what that looks like. So, the

85
00:08:49,720 --> 00:08:57,400
basic idea of KERI, and we'll go back into
this in more detail, but the idea is that

86
00:08:57,400 --> 00:09:01,980
we can define an identifier system
security overlay. It's a new type of

87
00:09:01,980 --> 00:09:07,060
overlay that acts as the trust spanning
layer. So, you have infrastructure that

88
00:09:07,060 --> 00:09:14,720
sits below the secure identifier overlay
or the secure identity overlay. And then

89
00:09:14,720 --> 00:09:21,000
you have a trust domain that sits above
that. And the spanning layer is that

90
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:26,920
overlay. Now, when people look at KERI,
often they look at it and it's difficult

91
00:09:26,920 --> 00:09:31,840
to understand. And one of the reasons that
KERI is difficult to understand is that we

92
00:09:31,840 --> 00:09:35,680
call it the platypus of the identity
space. And the reason for that is if you

93
00:09:35,680 --> 00:09:40,760
look at the platypus, it's a very
interesting animal. It's difficult to

94
00:09:40,760 --> 00:09:45,880
classify because it has a unique
combination of features not found

95
00:09:45,880 --> 00:09:50,500
elsewhere in nature. or at least not in
that combination. For example, because it

96
00:09:50,500 --> 00:09:55,880
has milk, a platypus looks like a mammal,
but it also lays eggs, so it looks like a

97
00:09:55,880 --> 00:10:02,820
reptile. It has a bill, so it looks like a
bird. It has electrolocation, so it looks

98
00:10:02,820 --> 00:10:10,600
like a ray. And it has venom, so it looks
like a snake. And so if you approach a

99
00:10:10,600 --> 00:10:15,980
platypus from the mindset that every
identity system has to look like a mammal,

100
00:10:15,980 --> 00:10:23,320
it will be confusing. Or if you approach
it from any of the other viewpoints. So

101
00:10:23,320 --> 00:10:29,980
KERI is best approached from the fact that
it is truly unique in many important ways.

102
00:10:30,260 --> 00:10:37,800
So KERI deconstructs what many people are
familiar with in identity and security

103
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:43,380
systems to create a novel combination of
properties that solve what we call the

104
00:10:43,380 --> 00:10:47,800
universal secure attribution problem. And
we'll go into that in more detail. If you

105
00:10:47,800 --> 00:10:52,180
think about a system design trade space,
you can have lots of puzzle pieces that

106
00:10:52,180 --> 00:10:57,380
you can put together and there's lots that
one can select from. But KERI carefully

107
00:10:57,380 --> 00:11:04,660
selected a subset and left certain things
out that it didn't need so that it got the

108
00:11:04,660 --> 00:11:10,840
properties that it did need to solve the
problem and the problem of a trust

109
00:11:10,840 --> 00:11:15,820
spanning layer requires solving the
problem of universal secure attribution.

110
00:11:15,820 --> 00:11:21,400
And if you think about it, if I want a
trust spanning layer, then I have to be

111
00:11:21,400 --> 00:11:28,520
able to do something universally. So KERI
is a well-fit-together puzzle where the

112
00:11:28,520 --> 00:11:35,320
pieces are carefully designed to match
that trade space. So let's step back a

113
00:11:35,320 --> 00:11:41,700
little bit because we've talked about the
internet. Let's talk about each individual

114
00:11:41,700 --> 00:11:47,820
person. Why should I care about having a
trust spanning layer on the internet. Why

115
00:11:47,820 --> 00:11:54,840
should it matter to me? The fundamental
idea that KERI tries to solve is to enable

116
00:11:54,840 --> 00:12:01,020
entities, both natural persons and legal
entities of all types, to retake control

117
00:12:01,020 --> 00:12:07,080
of our data and the value of that data.
And if you think about the internet and

118
00:12:07,080 --> 00:12:11,900
how it's evolved, most of the value of the
data on the internet is not controlled by

119
00:12:11,900 --> 00:12:16,720
the individuals that source the data or or
subjects of the data, but they're

120
00:12:16,720 --> 00:12:23,080
controlled by large internet companies,
super aggregators, super data correlators.

121
00:12:23,280 --> 00:12:27,100
They control the value of the internet.
People gain a lot of value of

122
00:12:27,100 --> 00:12:32,700
interactions, but many of those
interactions are designed to maximize

123
00:12:32,700 --> 00:12:41,560
engagement and often engagement is
maximized through rage or other deplorable

124
00:12:41,560 --> 00:12:50,600
human type interactions. And so we want to
have high value interactions on the

125
00:12:50,600 --> 00:12:55,060
internet that are valuable to the people
interacting. They're not being exploited.

126
00:12:55,300 --> 00:12:59,620
So when you think about building a system,
you think about, well, how do I build

127
00:12:59,620 --> 00:13:04,280
something? What are my tools? What are the
toolkits available to me? And so if my

128
00:13:04,280 --> 00:13:09,540
toolkit, and these are tools from my tool
chest in the garage, these are all

129
00:13:09,540 --> 00:13:16,600
hammers, various types of hammers. and
they work really well with nails. If I

130
00:13:16,600 --> 00:13:20,540
have a set of screws, those hammers aren't
very useful. I need a different toolkit. I

131
00:13:20,540 --> 00:13:25,800
need screwdrivers. So we need to figure
out what our toolkit is because actually

132
00:13:25,800 --> 00:13:33,800
in security systems, we don't have a
choice of toolkits. We've got one. So what

133
00:13:33,800 --> 00:13:38,640
we have to do is we have to figure out how
to make it work with that one toolkit and

134
00:13:38,640 --> 00:13:44,380
we have to be able to build our systems
with just one So if our toolkit is

135
00:13:44,380 --> 00:13:47,160
screwdrivers, we've got to build
everything with screws. If our toolkit is

136
00:13:47,160 --> 00:13:50,500
hammers, we have to build everything with
nails. So there's really only one set of

137
00:13:50,500 --> 00:13:55,040
tools for truly secure data control. We
only have one. And so we have to figure

138
00:13:55,040 --> 00:14:02,680
out how to best use that one. And I would
say that the existing internet security

139
00:14:02,680 --> 00:14:10,860
systems don't do a really good job of the
existing toolkit. Now, fair enough to say

140
00:14:10,860 --> 00:14:16,720
that 20 years ago, ago, the toolkit was
even worse than it is now. They had fewer

141
00:14:16,720 --> 00:14:21,540
tools and they did the best they could
with the set of tools they had then. But

142
00:14:21,540 --> 00:14:27,360
we have better tools now, but it's still
of the same type of tools and we could do

143
00:14:27,360 --> 00:14:32,120
better than they did. And so KERI is about
recognizing that the tools have gotten

144
00:14:32,120 --> 00:14:38,360
enough better that we can do significantly
better in our toolkit. And those tools are

145
00:14:38,360 --> 00:14:43,500
entropy-derived tools. So these are
primarily cryptographic one-way functions.

146
00:14:43,920 --> 00:14:47,140
So these are things like cryptographic
strength pseudorandom number generators,

147
00:14:47,880 --> 00:14:54,940
hashes or digests, the ECC key pair
generation algorithms, digital signatures,

148
00:14:55,160 --> 00:15:01,760
encryption. Now, in a few years, we're
going to have to add another set of tools

149
00:15:01,760 --> 00:15:07,600
because quantum computers are going to
provide the ability to tax ECC key pair

150
00:15:07,600 --> 00:15:13,040
generation. And so we need to have post
-quantum key pair generation algorithms,

151
00:15:13,200 --> 00:15:21,160
which NIST has now approved. So it has to
be on a roadmap. But for today, we can

152
00:15:21,160 --> 00:15:28,040
just focus and KERI was designed five
years ago. So we can focus on that, but

153
00:15:28,040 --> 00:15:32,840
KERI is cryptographically agile. So KERI
doesn't have already has a migration path

154
00:15:32,840 --> 00:15:39,780
to be post-quantum secure. So let's talk a
little bit about the economics of data. As

155
00:15:39,780 --> 00:15:46,660
I said, we want to we want to be able to
retake control of our data

156
00:15:46,660 --> 00:15:54,920
and its value. So when you think about
economics, it's about value, right? And

157
00:15:54,920 --> 00:16:00,520
it's about the creation and capture of
value. And creation and capture looks like

158
00:16:00,520 --> 00:16:07,320
extraction, exchange, and exploitation,
both in bad and good ways. And so if we

159
00:16:07,320 --> 00:16:13,500
want to take control over the value of our
data, data, then we need some way to

160
00:16:13,500 --> 00:16:18,600
secure that control. So the goal is
control over the value. And there are two

161
00:16:18,600 --> 00:16:24,000
types of value. There are its, these are
atoms. People are built out of atoms. So

162
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:31,340
you can think of people as value of people
as also atoms. We have things built out of

163
00:16:31,340 --> 00:16:39,680
atoms, buildings, cars, things. And to
secure those atoms, we have something

164
00:16:39,680 --> 00:16:43,880
called physical security. And there are
lots of physical security systems and

165
00:16:43,880 --> 00:16:51,680
tools for physical security. And for the
most part, it's pretty difficult to attack

166
00:16:51,680 --> 00:16:58,620
physical security. It usually involves
putting

167
00:16:58,620 --> 00:17:05,680
lives at risk to ultimately gain physical
control over atoms that are protected.

168
00:17:06,080 --> 00:17:11,640
Physical security is largely a solved
problem. The other type of value are bits.

169
00:17:11,880 --> 00:17:18,860
This is information value. And the problem
with information is that it's digital and

170
00:17:18,860 --> 00:17:25,440
can be copied. So you have documents, you
have places to store data, but you can

171
00:17:25,440 --> 00:17:32,020
copy information at will indefinitely
without losing any of its fidelity.

172
00:17:32,220 --> 00:17:35,880
Whereas we don't yet know how to copy
atoms. We don't yet know how to clone

173
00:17:35,880 --> 00:17:42,820
people. It requires new atoms. You can
build things that look like old things,

174
00:17:42,940 --> 00:17:49,160
but they're different things. And the cost
of building them is high. Whereas

175
00:17:49,160 --> 00:17:55,640
information, to make a digital copy of
information, the cost is extremely low.

176
00:17:57,280 --> 00:18:03,280
And so information security is by nature a
different type of security because we

177
00:18:03,280 --> 00:18:09,820
can't have a single copy that we protect
physically. We have multiple copies. And

178
00:18:09,820 --> 00:18:16,180
once those copies leak out, then they're
everywhere. So, and then the question is,

179
00:18:16,300 --> 00:18:22,400
you know, what does that look like? You
know, from the standpoint of people, you

180
00:18:22,400 --> 00:18:32,200
know, how do people control, you know, how
do we have information security? So, we

181
00:18:32,200 --> 00:18:38,100
want control over value. We have the two
types of security. And the one thing that

182
00:18:38,100 --> 00:18:43,660
we have, in our toolkit, and remember I
said they're entropy-derived tools, is

183
00:18:43,660 --> 00:18:49,920
that we can have digital uniqueness. It's
a very narrow type of digital uniqueness

184
00:18:49,920 --> 00:18:59,560
and it's called entropy. And basically we
can create numbers that are derived

185
00:18:59,560 --> 00:19:05,540
randomly such that somebody can't guess
the number as long as the number is kept

186
00:19:05,540 --> 00:19:10,220
secret. So if we don't share the number,
they can't guess it. And that is the only

187
00:19:10,220 --> 00:19:15,720
security that we have. Everything else is
one-way functions that try to preserve

188
00:19:15,720 --> 00:19:21,240
that original entropy, that original level
of cryptographic security that says they

189
00:19:21,240 --> 00:19:30,120
can't guess the number. And so that's not
a very rich tool set. So now how do we

190
00:19:30,120 --> 00:19:39,340
control things? Well, we as people, we are
its. We want to control bits. How do we do

191
00:19:39,340 --> 00:19:44,920
that? Well, the way we do that is that we
create cryptographic pseudonymous

192
00:19:44,920 --> 00:19:53,360
identifiers that are derived from entropy
and we build a mechanism that allows us to

193
00:19:53,360 --> 00:19:59,920
prove that we control those identifiers.
We're not talking about all the

194
00:19:59,920 --> 00:20:04,660
information in the world. We're talking
about just identifiers. We can control

195
00:20:04,660 --> 00:20:11,200
identifiers. And if we can control
identifiers, And then if we have a way to

196
00:20:11,200 --> 00:20:16,800
make attribution to information to an
identifier, then we can at least assert

197
00:20:16,800 --> 00:20:25,760
that the source of the information came
from the person or entity that controlled

198
00:20:25,760 --> 00:20:30,720
